This is a review of the current uncommitted change to
arch/x86/mm/pat/set_memory.c on branch direct-map/x86-cpa-based/v0.1.

Author: Mike Rapoport (Microsoft) <rppt@kernel.org>

set_memory: factor large page handling out of __change_page_attr()

The change moves the large page check and split out of
__change_page_attr() into a new helper handle_large_page().

> diff --git a/arch/x86/mm/pat/set_memory.c b/arch/x86/mm/pat/set_memory.c
> index 76109ebf1594..774247d15115 100644
> --- a/arch/x86/mm/pat/set_memory.c
> +++ b/arch/x86/mm/pat/set_memory.c
> @@ -1249,6 +1249,30 @@ static int split_large_page(struct cpa_data *cpa, pte_t *kpte,
>  	return 0;
>  }
>  
> +static int handle_large_page(pte_t *kpte, unsigned long address,
> +			     struct cpa_data *cpa)
> +{
> +	int do_split;
> +
> +	/*
> +	 * Check, whether we can keep the large page intact
> +	 * and just change the pte:
> +	 */
> +	do_split = should_split_large_page(kpte, address, cpa);
> +	/*
> +	 * When the range fits into the existing large page,
> +	 * return. cp->numpages and cpa->tlbflush have been updated in
> +	 * try_large_page:
> +	 */
> +	if (do_split <= 0)
> +		return do_split;
> +
> +	/*
> +	 * We have to split the large page:
> +	 */
> +	return split_large_page(cpa, kpte, address);
> +}

handle_large_page() returns 0 for two different outcomes:

  - should_split_large_page() returned 0, meaning the large page was kept
    and only the pte was updated in place (or nothing changed at all).

  - should_split_large_page() returned > 0 and split_large_page() then
    succeeded, also returning 0.

The caller can no longer tell these apart.  Was that intended?

