> -	err = split_large_page(cpa, kpte, address);
> +	err = handle_large_page(kpte, address, cpa);
>  	if (!err)
>  		goto repeat;

Before this change, should_split_large_page() returning 0 made
__change_page_attr() return 0 right away:

	do_split = should_split_large_page(kpte, address, cpa);
	if (do_split <= 0)
		return do_split;

Now that 0 comes back as err == 0, and "if (!err) goto repeat;" jumps to
the repeat label instead of returning.

should_split_large_page() returns 0 on the common "keep the large page"
path.  __should_split_large_page() returns 0 when the requested prot
already matches:

	if (pgprot_val(req_prot) == pgprot_val(old_prot)) {
		cpa_inc_lp_sameprot(level);
		return 0;
	}

and again after updating the large page in place:

	/* All checks passed. Update the large page mapping. */
	new_pte = pfn_pte(old_pfn, new_prot);
	__set_pmd_pte(kpte, address, new_pte);
	cpa->flags |= CPA_FLUSHTLB;
	cpa_inc_lp_preserved(level);
	return 0;

On "goto repeat", address is unchanged (it is computed once before the
repeat label) and the large page was not split, so _lookup_address_cpa()
finds the same large page and should_split_large_page() returns 0 again.
Does __change_page_attr() then spin here forever?

__change_page_attr() is called with cpa_lock held from
__change_page_attr_set_clr():

	if (!debug_pagealloc_enabled())
		spin_lock(&cpa_lock);
	ret = __change_page_attr(cpa, primary);

so would a set_memory_*() call that lands on a preserved 2M or 1G mapping
hang the cpu with cpa_lock held?

The original code only reached "goto repeat" after an actual split.  Would
gating the repeat on the split outcome, rather than on err == 0, keep that
behavior, e.g.:

	do_split = should_split_large_page(kpte, address, cpa);
	if (do_split <= 0)
		return do_split;

	err = split_large_page(cpa, kpte, address);
	if (!err)
		goto repeat;

	return err;

or by having handle_large_page() report "split done, please repeat"
distinctly from "large page kept, done"?
